# The sprite bundles ARE committed, and that is deliberate. They have to be
# served same-origin from GitHub Pages: release assets send no CORS headers at
# all, so a browser can never fetch() one, however convenient it would be to
# keep them out of the repo. Tested with an Origin header; there is no header
# to be had. Pages sends access-control-allow-origin: *.

# The loose per-species sprite packs are build INTERMEDIATES, not artefacts.
# They are what pack_bundle.py reads to build web/sprites-*.pak, and the .pak
# files are what actually gets distributed -- so tracking both puts the same
# ~105 MB of sprite data in the repo twice. Regenerate with:
#
#   python3 tools/pack_pmd.py            # or: kanto / johto / hoenn
#   python3 tools/make_thumbs.py
#
# CREDITS.md already recommends exactly this: ship the code and the scripts and
# let each user package the sprites from the original sources.
tools/sdcard/mons/*.bin

# Pokemon cries (tools/pack_cries.py). The audio is (c) The Pokemon Company, so
# like the avatars it is fetched by a script rather than committed. web/cries.pak
# is ignored until the owner decides to publish it -- remove that line to let
# the Pages installer offer the button.
tools/cries_cache/
tools/sdcard/cries/
web/cries.pak

# Emulator save files and downloaded generator intermediates: local scratch, not
# artefacts. They were committed by accident once.
tamapoke.nvs
tamapoke.nvs.*
tools/_*.svg
backups/
